#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153360 :tutorial/content "For any two integers $a$ and $b,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b).\n$$\n\nThis is known as **Bézout's identity**. \n\nTo find the integers $u$ and $v,$ we can use the **extended Euclidean algorithm**, which involves the following three steps:\n\n- Carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b).$\n\n- Solve for the remainder at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder.\n\n- Back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $au+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b).$\n\nFor example, let's use the extended Euclidean algorithm to find integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n48u+18v= \\textrm{gcd}(48,18).\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}48 & = & 18\\cdot 2 & + & 12 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 18 & = & 12\\cdot 1 & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 12 & = & 6\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we find that $\\text{gcd}(48,18) =6.$\n\n- **Step 2:** We solve for the remainder (the rightmost term) at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as an integer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =48-18\\cdot 2 \\\\ 6 & =18-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $48u+18v= 6.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =18-12 \\\\  & =18-(48-18\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =18-48+18\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(18+18\\cdot 2)-48 \\\\  & =18\\cdot 3-48 \\\\  & =48\\cdot (-1)+18\\cdot (3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6f364b85-4d93-5a09-a174-dea640e1c448" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4024 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153483 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson we've made use of **Bézout's identity**, which states that for any two integers $a$ and $b,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b).\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that any integer combination of $a$ and $b$ is also a multiple of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b).$ In other words, for *any* integers $u'$ and $v',$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(a,b) \\mid (au'+bv')\n$$\n\nMoreover, the set of integers \n\n$$\n\\big\\{  au'+bv' \\,:\\, u', v' \\in\\mathbb Z \\big \\}\n$$\n\ncontains *all* the multiples of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b).$\n\nWe'll make use of these results in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "9505be6e-ec53-5537-bf81-d4b7b197c384" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9653 :tutorial/title "Final Remarks"}]}
